48 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df1e1d9e-0dc1-5725-b77d-a7edfadad3f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512851 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e318cf23-99c3-5de3-b262-09a09b316069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512869 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512869 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512869 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512869 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512869 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b02c01d-81c9-557d-8302-9b53cb371ef3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512869 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ | $0.75$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.75+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1.5+1 \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+1.2 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512880 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512880 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512880 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{14}{25}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{19}{15},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512880 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28314126-6eaa-5326-a420-9e962fb3a315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512880 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{25}y+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{25}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{15}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}-(\\frac{14}{25})(\\frac{19}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512882 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.65$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ce015c9-6f9e-5825-a2d1-d00ea38bdb08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512882 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.65$ | $0.65$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.35+2\\cdot 0.65 \\\\  & =0+1.3 \\\\  & =1.3 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.9 \\\\  & =0.1+3.6 \\\\  & =3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.3)(y-3.7)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.3)(1-3.7)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(0-1.3)(4-3.7)f(0,4) \\\\  & +(2-1.3)(1-3.7)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.3)(4-3.7)f(2,4) \\\\  & =(-1.3)(-2.7)(0.1) \\\\  & +(-1.3)(0.3)(0.25) \\\\  & +(0.7)(-2.7)(0) \\\\  & +(0.7)(0.3)(0.65) \\\\  & =0.39.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512888 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512888 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512888 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{13}{10}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{13}{10},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512888 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57775fd5-4d0c-5406-bb3e-6af6db58e9c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512888 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y[\\frac{32}{5}+2{y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{40}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-(\\frac{13}{10})(\\frac{13}{10}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512897 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512897 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512897 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512897 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72eb6c64-1cf3-5dde-b025-69a7158cf18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512897 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.65$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0+1.2 \\\\  & =1.2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.65+3\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =0.65+1.05 \\\\  & =1.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.2)(y-1.7)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.2)(1-1.7)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(0-1.2)(3-1.7)f(0,3) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(1-1.7)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(3-1.7)f(2,3) \\\\  & =(-1.2)(-0.7)(0.15) \\\\  & +(-1.2)(1.3)(0.25) \\\\  & +(0.8)(-0.7)(0.5) \\\\  & +(0.8)(1.3)(0.1) \\\\  & =-0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512899 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.12$ | $0.28$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.18$ | $0.12$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7601220c-590a-55f7-9ebc-4ae99b2759bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512899 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.12$ | $0.28$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.18$ | $0.12$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.5+4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.3+5\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.3+0.9+2 \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3)(y-3.2)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512905 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512905 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147964" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512905 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512905 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$\n\n*Hint: It can be shown that $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible values of $x$ and $y.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88c40e55-9afb-513d-8399-c6e943bf9ded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512905 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\nP(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values of $x$ and $y.$ Therefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent. \n\nSince $X$ are $Y$ are independent, we have $\\text{Cov}(X,Y)=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512912 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512912 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512912 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512912 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=1.3$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.9,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512912 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9576e0da-b7f3-5620-ad1a-43d5569461f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512912 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0)(1)(0.15)+(0)(3)(0.2)+(2)(1)(0.4)+(2)(3)(0.25) \\\\  & =2.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2.3-(1.3)(1.9) \\\\  & =-0.17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512918 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.5$ | $0$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a725de9b-9a65-5d96-80a3-b82e15275c01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512918 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+3\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5+1.5 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =1.4+1.2 \\\\  & =2.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2)(y-2.6)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-2)(2-2.6)f(1,2) \\\\  & +(1-2)(4-2.6)f(1,4) \\\\  & +(3-2)(2-2.6)f(3,2) \\\\  & +(3-2)(4-2.6)f(3,4) \\\\  & =(-1)(-0.6)(0.5) \\\\  & +(-1)(1.4)(0) \\\\  & +(1)(-0.6)(0.2) \\\\  & +(1)(1.4)(0.3) \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512926 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1903a33-4088-5de3-962d-d8462d93f10a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512926 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0+0.5 \\\\  & =0.5 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+1.2 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-0.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512928 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=4$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.4,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b781bcca-e045-541a-9fb8-bd56d31a763e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512928 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)(0.2)+(2)(2)(0.1)+(4)(1)(0.2) \\\\  & +(4)(2)(0.2)+(6)(1)(0.2)+(6)(2)(0.1) \\\\  & =5.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =5.6-(4)(1.4) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512941 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512941 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512941 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512941 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{7}{12}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512941 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf31f09e-0f29-56f8-9f97-abccd7a0062d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512941 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512944 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4d126ea-cebf-548f-a0ce-ca7561957246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512944 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.8+1.8 \\\\  & =2.6 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.7+2\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.7+0.6 \\\\  & =1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.6)(y-1.3)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2-2.6)(1-1.3)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-2.6)(2-1.3)f(2,2) \\\\  & +(3-2.6)(1-1.3)f(3,1) \\\\  & +(3-2.6)(2-1.3)f(3,2) \\\\  & =(-0.6)(-0.3)(0.3) \\\\  & +(-0.6)(0.7)(0.1) \\\\  & +(0.4)(-0.3)(0.4) \\\\  & +(0.4)(0.7)(0.2) \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512946 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=3.2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=3.8,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de144ac2-d89b-5e69-8e47-de2554ce330e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512946 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(3)(0.25)+(2)(5)(0.15)+(4)(3)(0.35)+(4)(5)(0.25) \\\\  & =12.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =12.2-(3.2)(3.8) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512952 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512952 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512952 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef29076a-1f6b-53fd-9d0b-390107f2fcd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512952 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S